// The terms page and the two legal checkboxes. The page is plain words in the
// hush voice; the checkboxes are the operative part — both are enforced
// server-side and recorded (agreements.go), the labels just tell the truth
// about what checking them means.
package main

// agreeBox is the 18+/terms checkbox the three signup doors share. Required
// client-side AND server-side (agreed + recordAgreement) — the HTML attribute
// is a courtesy, the handler is the rule.
templ agreeBox() {
	<label class="hush-agree">
		<input type="checkbox" name="agree18" required/>
		<span>i am 18 or older and i accept the <a class="hush-link" href="/terms" target="_blank">terms</a>.</span>
	</label>
}

// certBox2257 is the certification that rides every image upload — the wall
// form and the camera page both carry it. id'd so camera.js can read it.
templ certBox2257(id string) {
	<label class="hush-agree">
		<input type="checkbox" id={ id } name="cert2257" required/>
		<span>i certify under 18 U.S.C. § 2257 that every person depicted is 18 or older, and that i am the producer of this image or hold the records proving it. see the <a class="hush-link" href="/terms" target="_blank">terms</a>.</span>
	</label>
}

// termsPage is /terms: the rules of the wall, written the way the wall talks.
// contact is the operator address (HUSH_ADMIN_EMAIL) — shown when set.
templ termsPage(contact string) {
	@shell("hush — terms") {
		<div class="hush-top hush-terms">
			<header class="hush-head">
				<h1 class="hush-title"><a class="hush-home" href="/">hush</a></h1>
				<p class="hush-sub">terms of service</p>
			</header>
			<section class="hush-terms-body">
				<h2>1. what this is</h2>
				<p>hush is a curated, anonymous wall. you post into it; the wall decides — quietly — what stays. using it means you accept these terms. if you don't accept them, don't use it.</p>
				<h2>2. who may contribute</h2>
				<p>you must be <strong>18 years of age or older</strong> to hold an account or contribute anything — posts, images, votes, flags. by creating an account you represent that you are 18 or older. accounts held by anyone under 18 are terminated on discovery, and their content is purged.</p>
				<h2>3. accounts and the gate</h2>
				<p>accounts are granted, not owed: by application, by open seat, or by a member's invite. an inviter answers for who they bring. we may approve, reject, suspend, throttle, or terminate any account at any time, with or without a reason, with or without notice. anonymity on the wall is a design choice, not a promise of anonymity to us — see section 7.</p>
				<h2>4. your content</h2>
				<p>what you post stays yours. by posting it you grant us a worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free license to host, store, reproduce, resize, and display it as part of operating the wall. you represent that you have every right needed to post what you post.</p>
				<h2>5. what may not be posted</h2>
				<p>nothing illegal. in particular and without limitation: no content involving anyone under 18 in any sexualized context — <strong>zero tolerance; such material is removed, preserved as evidence, and reported to the national center for missing &amp; exploited children and to law enforcement</strong> — no content you lack rights to, no doxxing, no threats, no spam, no malware, nothing posted to harass. we may remove anything for any reason; the wall is curated by design.</p>
				<h2>6. images and 18 U.S.C. § 2257</h2>
				<p>every image upload requires a certification, which we record: that every person depicted was 18 years of age or older when the image was created, and that you are the producer of the image or hold the records required by 18 U.S.C. § 2257 and 28 C.F.R. part 75 proving it. uploading without being able to stand behind that certification is a violation of these terms and, where the law says so, a crime — yours, not ours.</p>
				<h2>7. what we collect</h2>
				<p>the wall shows no names, no scores, no counts. we still keep operational records: the ip address, approximate location, and browser signature attached to every post, vote, flag, signup, and certification; your optional email if you give one; and event analytics about how the wall is used. these records exist for moderation, abuse, security, and legal compliance, are visible to the operators, and are disclosed when the law compels it. we don't sell them.</p>
				<h2>8. moderation</h2>
				<p>members flag; trusted flags hide content immediately; operators review, restore, or purge. moderation decisions are not explained, appealable, or consistent, and we owe you none of those.</p>
				<h2>9. no warranty, no liability</h2>
				<p>the wall is provided as-is and as-available, with no warranties of any kind, express or implied. to the maximum extent the law allows, we are not liable for any indirect, incidental, special, consequential, or punitive damages, or for lost data, lost standing, or lost invites; our total liability for anything arising from the wall is limited to one hundred dollars or the amount you paid us — which is nothing.</p>
				<h2>10. changes and termination</h2>
				<p>we may change these terms, or shut the wall, at any time. continued use after a change is acceptance of it. sections 4 through 9 survive any termination.</p>
				if contact != "" {
					<h2>11. contact</h2>
					<p>operator and records contact: { contact }</p>
				}
			</section>
			@backToWall()
		</div>
	}
}
